- net
- pass

' This is the foot at /io/mesa, the one place where a world connects to Mesa.
' The foot holds the engine of the world and the key that the world signs
' with. The engine and the key are not records. The foot keeps no data that a
' restart cannot make again. The first time that a reader asks for a page, the
' foot derives that page from the history of its world. What the foot holds of
' other worlds is their mounts and the cards beside those mounts.
'
' These are the rules of the foot. The rules are steps that use the state of
' the foot and the state of the world. The actor that owns the socket runs the
' rules and admits all that the rules ask it to land.
'
' A page is the sequence of bytes that Mesa binds to one name and sends as
' fragments. The foot binds one page for each epic, for each pin, and for
' each slice of a page that is too long (mesa_foot/bind).
'
' An audience is the reader that a published root is for. A world publishes
' one root to each peer, /<self>/our/<peer>, and one root to all the peers
' that it knows, /<self>/our/public. The audience of a page from the root of
' a peer is the number of that peer. The audience of a page from the public
' root is 0. The function mesa_foot/mine gives the root of an audience, and
' the key that seals a page comes from the audience (mesa_foot/lock_of).
'
' The name of a page is publ/<kind>|<audience>|<x>|<y>. The audience is the
' number of the world that a root is published to, or 0 for the public root.
'   e|aud|base|head   an epic of that root from version base (0: from
'                     no version) to version head, without its records
'   p|aud|head|hash   the pin with that hash, shown in the epic at head
'   s|aud|page|n      slice n of a page too long for Mesa to send as one
'   h|aud|seq|head    not a page: the name in a hint. It gives the version
'                     of a root and the held statement of an identity log
' A version is a stamp (net/stamp). Thus a name refers to the same content at
' all times, and a page does not change after the foot binds it.
'
' A world seals for one peer all that it publishes to that peer. The two
' worlds share a key that each world derives from its own seed and the public
' key of the other world. The world seals a page of a peer's root with that
' key and binds the page to its name. A reader asks for a pin of that root by
' a keyed alias of the hash of the pin. Thus a watcher of the wire does not
' learn a record, and does not learn if it guessed a record correctly.
'
' A world sends public content as it is. Mesa signs each page, sealed or not
' sealed.
+ mesa_foot
  + limits
    ' These are the sizes of a packet, a name and a fragment: the defaults of
    ' Mesa. The signed-public profile sets a fragment to 1 KiB and a page to
    ' 1024 fragments. Thus a longer name is possible only with a larger
    ' packet. A larger value thus goes as pins, and each pin is one page at
    ' most.
    ^ row[nat]
    [1472 256 1024]
  + domain
    ' This is the domain that each name is in, for now. In Mesa, the domain of
    ' a name is a part of the identity of the name. This domain is a
    ' placeholder until worlds have names in a real domain.
    ^ nat
    | mesa_foot/tag "gw-btc"
  + realm
    ' This is what the foot tells the engine about each world: the domain,
    ' life 1 and rift 0. The life and the rift of Mesa are not used. The life
    ' and the rift of a name are in its log (pass.foil) and in each stamp
    ' (net/stamp). The kernel itself examines the log and each stamp. An
    ' engine holds no durable data and is made again each time a key changes.
    ' Thus an engine does not see a second life.
    ^ row[nat]
    [mesa_foot/domain 1 0]
  + bounds
    ' This row has the bounds of the engine, with the names that Mesa gives
    ' them: max_bindings, max_binding_bytes, max_reassembly_bytes and
    ' max_reads. These bounds are larger than their defaults, and those
    ' defaults are for a small number of pages. Then the row has the retry
    ' policy of Mesa, at its defaults: retry_after, read_timeout and
    ' max_attempts. The values retry_after and read_timeout are in
    ' milliseconds, the unit that the foot uses when it moves the engine
    ' forward. An engine holds 64 MiB of bound pages, the maximum that the
    ' bridge lets it hold. After that, the foot starts a new engine.
    ^ row[nat]
    [65536 67108864 67108864 1024 100 2000 4]
  + most_read
    ' This is the maximum number of bytes that the wire accepts from one
    ' connection.
    ^ nat
    16777216
  + acceptors
    ' This is the number of connections that the wire accepts at the same
    ' time.
    ^ nat
    4
  + every
    ' This is the number of beats of the wire's clock between two times that
    ' the foot tells its peers the versions that it has (tick). The clock has
    ' one beat each second.
    ^ nat
    15
  + name
    ' This function gives the name of a page in the signed-public space of
    ' Mesa: publ/<kind>|<a>|<b>|<c>. The name has one letter and three
    ' numbers. The place where each kind is used tells what the letter and the
    ' numbers mean.
    \ kind=nat a=nat b=nat c=nat
    ^ nat
    | mesa_foot/tag (str/to_atom (str/cat_all ["publ/" (str/from_atom kind) "|" (nat/show a) "|" (nat/show b) "|" (nat/show c)]))
  + parse
    ' This function gives [kind a b c] from the name of a page. It gives no
    ' parts when the input is not the name of a page.
    \ named=nat
    ^ row[nat]
    = none=row[nat] []
    ? (lt (wid named) 9) none
    = text (str/from_atom (mod named (bex (mul 8 (dec (wid named))))))
      parts (str/split_byte 124 text)
    ? !(eq parts.len 4) none
    = head parts.at(0)
    ? !(eq head.len 6) none
    ? !(eq (str/slice 0 5 head) "publ/") none
    ?> (./some a) (nat/parse parts.at(1)) none
    ?> (./some b) (nat/parse parts.at(2)) none
    ?> (./some c) (nat/parse parts.at(3)) none
    [head.at(5) a b c]
  + most
    ' These are the maximum number of streams that one epic can make a reader
    ' get, and the maximum number of bytes.
    ^ nat
    65536
  + room
    ^ nat
    268435456
  + page
    ' This is the maximum number of bytes that the foot sends as one page. It
    ' is below the 1 MiB limit of Mesa, and that gives space for a seal.
    ^ nat
    1000000
  + most_bytes
    ' This is the maximum number of bytes that one page can have, in slices.
    ^ nat
    67108864
  + peer
    ' This is a world that this world corresponds with. It holds the name of
    ' that world, the number that Mesa knows it by, and the key that it signs
    ' with.
    : world=iota id=nat key=nat
  + ask
    ' This is a page that the foot asked for and that still has no answer. It
    ' holds the peer that the foot asked and the parts of the name of the
    ' page. The field want is the content hash that the bytes of a pin must
    ' have. The name contains only an alias of that hash.
    : request=nat from=nat kind=nat aud=nat a=nat b=nat want=nat
  + stream
    ' These are the bytes of a pin as they came. The foot did a check of the
    ' bytes against the hash that named them.
    : hash=nat bytes=nat
  + load
    ' This is an epic of the root of one publisher while the foot gathers it.
    ' It holds:
    ' - the streams that came for the epic
    ' - the hashes that the foot asked for and that still have no answer
    ' - the hashes that the foot must still ask for
    '
    ' The foot asks for only a window of these hashes at one time.
    : from=nat aud=nat epic=net/epic streams=row[mesa_foot/stream] wanted=row[nat] queued=row[nat] size=nat
  + lock
    ' This is what this world shares with one peer. It holds a key to seal
    ' with, a key to make nonces from, and a key to make aliases of names
    ' with. All these keys are zero when the key of the peer is not a key.
    : id=nat seal=nat nonce=nat name=nat
  + long
    ' This is a page that comes in slices. It holds the ask that the page is
    ' the answer to, the length of the page, and the slices that came until
    ' now.
    : ask=mesa_foot/ask mark=nat total=nat count=nat asked=nat got=row[mesa_foot/stream]
  + window
    ' This is the maximum number of pages of one epic that the foot asks for
    ' at one time.
    ^ nat
    16
  + shown
    ' This is a pin that this world showed to an audience in an epic that it
    ' supplied.
    : bytes=nat
  + landing
    ' This is a landing that the foot asked the world to admit.
    : request=nat from=nat aud=nat head=nat
  + effect
    ' This is what the foot asks the actor that runs it to do.
    & mesa_foot/effect/send
      mesa_foot/effect/admit
    + send
      : to=nat packet=nat
    + admit
      : request=nat plan={\ namespace}either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
  + foot
    : self=iota seed=nat rift=nat engine=nat peers=row[mesa_foot/peer] next=nat
      asks=row[mesa_foot/ask] loads=row[mesa_foot/load]
      lands=row[mesa_foot/landing]
      bound=mop[path nat lt_pith] shown=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
      told=mop[path nat lt_pith]
      locks=row[mesa_foot/lock] kept=mop[path nat lt_pith] longs=row[mesa_foot/long]
  + turn
    ' This is what a step gives: the foot, and what the foot asks the actor to
    ' do.
    : foot=mesa_foot/foot does=row[mesa_foot/effect]
  + id
    ' This function gives the number that Mesa knows a world by.
    \ world=iota
    ^ nat
    | quip_val world
  + start
    ' This function makes a foot for a world. The engine of the foot knows the
    ' key of each peer. This set of keys does not change for the life of the
    ' engine. Thus a new peer makes a new foot necessary. A new foot has no
    ' cost, because an engine holds no durable data.
    \ self=iota seed=nat rift=nat peers=row[mesa_foot/peer]
    ^ mesa_foot/foot
    = others=row[row[nat]]
        | peers.map
          \ item=mesa_foot/peer
          ^ row[nat]
          [item.id item.key]
      ;engine (MesaEngine (mesa_foot/id self) seed mesa_foot/realm mesa_foot/limits mesa_foot/bounds others)
      asks=row[mesa_foot/ask] []
      loads=row[mesa_foot/load] []
      lands=row[mesa_foot/landing] []
      bound=mop[path nat lt_pith] (mop/empty)
      shown=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith] (mop/empty)
      told=mop[path nat lt_pith] (mop/empty)
      kept=mop[path nat lt_pith] (mop/empty)
      longs=row[mesa_foot/long] []
      locks
        | peers.map
          \ item=mesa_foot/peer
          ^ mesa_foot/lock
          = shared (BoxKey seed item.key)
          ? (eq 0 shared) (mesa_foot/lock item.id 0 0 0)
          | mesa_foot/lock item.id (Blake3Keyed shared (mesa_foot/tag "seal")) (Blake3Keyed shared (mesa_foot/tag "nonce")) (Blake3Keyed shared (mesa_foot/tag "name"))
    | mesa_foot/foot self seed rift engine peers 1 asks loads lands bound shown told locks kept longs
  + tag
    ' This function gives a word as bytes.
    \ text=nat
    ^ nat
    | add text (bex (mul 8 (wid text)))
  + lock_of
    ' This function gives the keys that this world shares with a peer. It
    ' gives none when there are no keys to use.
    \ id=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ maybe[mesa_foot/lock]
    = none=maybe[mesa_foot/lock] .none
      found foot.locks.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/lock (eq item.id id)))
    ? !(found.len) none
    ? (eq 0 found.at(0).seal) none
    | ./some found.at(0)
  + veil
    ' This function gives the number that a reader uses to ask for a pin. For
    ' a public root, the number is the hash of the pin. For a peer's root, the
    ' number is a keyed alias of that hash.
    \ name=nat hash=nat
    ^ nat
    ? (eq 0 name) hash
    | Blake3Keyed name hash
  + hashlike
    ' This function tells if a number has the form of a content hash: 32
    ' bytes.
    \ h=nat
    ^ bool
    ? !(eq 33 (wid h)) .false
    | eq 1 (load8 32 h)
  + wrap
    ' This function gives a page that is sealed for a peer and bound to its
    ' name. It makes the nonce from the page and the name of the page, with a
    ' key that is for the nonce only. Thus the same page under the same name
    ' gives the same bytes. Thus also, the function does not use one nonce for
    ' two contents.
    \ with=mesa_foot/lock spelled=nat page=nat
    ^ nat
    = made (Blake3Keyed (Blake3Keyed with.nonce (Sha256 page)) spelled)
      raw (mod made (bex 192))
      sealed (SecretSeal with.seal (add raw (bex 192)) spelled page)
    | add raw (lsh sealed 192)
  + unwrap
    ' This function gives the page that is in sealed bytes. It gives 0 when
    ' this peer did not seal the bytes, when the seal is not for this name, or
    ' when the bytes were changed.
    \ with=mesa_foot/lock spelled=nat bytes=nat
    ^ nat
    ? (lt (wid bytes) 42) 0
    | SecretOpen with.seal (add (mod bytes (bex 192)) (bex 192)) spelled (rsh bytes 192)
  + renew
    ' This function gives the same foot with a new engine. The foot does this
    ' when the engine has no budget left for bound pages. The new foot derives
    ' a bound page again when a reader next asks for that page. The foot
    ' discards the reads that are in progress, and a later hint starts them
    ' again.
    \ foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/foot
    = fresh (mesa_foot/start foot.self foot.seed foot.rift foot.peers)
    | fresh.set_next(foot.next).set_lands(foot.lands).set_shown(foot.shown).set_told(foot.told).set_kept(foot.kept)
  + quiet
    \ foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = none=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
    | mesa_foot/turn foot none
  + peer_of
    \ id=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ maybe[mesa_foot/peer]
    = none=maybe[mesa_foot/peer] .none
      found foot.peers.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/peer (eq item.id id)))
    ? !(found.len) none
    | ./some found.at(0)
  + link
    ' This function gives the number that Mesa knows the link of a peer by.
    ' The number is the place of the peer among the peers.
    \ id=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    % i=nat 0
    ^ nat
    ? (ge i foot.peers.len) 0
    ? (eq foot.peers.at(i).id id) i.inc
    | _ i.inc
  + mine
    ' This function gives the root that this world publishes to an audience.
    \ aud=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ maybe[path]
    = none=maybe[path] .none
      public=path [foot.self 'our 'public]
    ? (eq aud 0) (./some public)
    ?> (./some item) (mesa_foot/peer_of aud foot) none
    = pairwise=path [foot.self 'our item.world]
    | ./some pairwise
  + theirs
    ' This function gives the root of a publisher as the publisher spells it.
    ' This is the root for this world, or the public root of the publisher.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer public=bool foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ path
    = open=path [from.world 'our 'public]
      pairwise=path [from.world 'our foot.self]
    ? public open
    pairwise
  + mount
    ' This function gives the path where this world holds the root of a
    ' publisher.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer public=bool foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ path
    = root (mesa_foot/theirs from public foot)
    | (net/mounted foot.self root).fall(root)
  + card
    ' This function gives the path where this world keeps the state of such a
    ' mount.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer public=bool foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ path
    = pairwise=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa from.world]
      open=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa from.world 'public]
    ? public open
    pairwise
  + head
    ' For a root that this world publishes, this function gives the version
    ' that the root is at. It gives 0 while the root holds no record.
    \ root=path state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    = case state.case(.z root).fall(0)
    ? (eq 0 case) 0
    | net/stamp foot.rift case
  + sends
    ' This function gives what an engine asks the foot to send.
    \ acts=row[row[nat]]
    ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
    = none=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
    | acts.fold none
      \ out=row[mesa_foot/effect] act=row[nat]
      ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
      ? !(eq 1 act.at(0)) out
      | out.snoc((mesa_foot/effect/send act.at(1) act.at(3)))
  + after
    ' This function does one turn and then the next turn, and gives the two as
    ' one turn.
    \ first=mesa_foot/turn next={\ mesa_foot/foot}mesa_foot/turn
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = second (next first.foot)
    | mesa_foot/turn second.foot first.does.weld(second.does)
  + epic
    ' This function gives the change of a root between two of its versions,
    ' when this world can give that change exactly. This is possible when the
    ' two versions are of this continuity and are in the history of this
    ' world.
    \ root=path base=nat head=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ maybe[net/epic]
    = w32 4294967296
      none=maybe[net/epic] .none
    ? !(eq (div head w32) foot.rift) none
    ? !((eq base 0) || (eq (div base w32) foot.rift)) none
    | net/delta foot.rift root (mod base w32) (mod head w32) state
  + spot
    \ aud=nat hash=nat
    ^ path
    [($u aud) ($u hash)]
  + parts
    ' This function gives a pin and each pin below it, as this world shows
    ' them to an audience. Each pin is under the number that a reader uses to
    ' ask for it.
    \ aud=nat name=nat top=pin[any] out=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
    ^ mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
    = at (mesa_foot/spot aud (mesa_foot/veil name (PinHash top)))
    ? out.has(at) out
    = kept out.put(at (mesa_foot/shown (PinBytes top)))
      inner=row[pin[any]] (PinParts top)
    | inner.fold kept
      \ acc=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith] part=pin[any]
      ^ mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
      | mesa_foot/parts aud name part acc
  + show
    ' This function keeps what an epic shows to its audience: each record that
    ' the epic names, and the pins that those records hold. The result is a
    ' cache. The foot derives again all that is not in the cache, from the
    ' head that a name contains.
    \ aud=nat name=nat epic=net/epic out=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
    ^ mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
    = fresh=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith] (mop/empty)
      room (? (gt out.size 16384) fresh out)
    | epic.entries.fold room
      \ acc=mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith] item=net/entry
      ^ mop[path mesa_foot/shown lt_pith]
      ?> (./some record) item.record acc
      = held=pin[any] (pin record)
      | mesa_foot/parts aud name held acc
  + served
    : foot=mesa_foot/foot acts=row[row[nat]]
  + serve
    ' This function binds the page that a name asks for, the first time that a
    ' reader asks for it. It does this when the page is one that this world
    ' can give as an answer. The function seals a page of a peer's root for
    ' that peer. If this world shares no key with that peer, the foot supplies
    ' no part of that root.
    \ name=row[nat] spelled=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/served
    = none=row[row[nat]] []
      idle (mesa_foot/served foot none)
      key=path [($u spelled)]
    ? foot.bound.has(key) idle
    = kind name.at(0)
      aud name.at(1)
      with (mesa_foot/lock_of aud foot)
    ? (and !(eq 0 aud) (maybe/is_none with)) idle
    = veiled
        ?> (./some held) with 0
        held.name
    ? (eq kind 115)
      ?> (./some whole) foot.kept.get((mesa_foot/spot aud name.at(2))) idle
      = at (mul name.at(3) mesa_foot/page)
        total (dec (wid whole))
      ? (ge at total) idle
      = len (? (lt (sub total at) mesa_foot/page) (sub total at) mesa_foot/page)
      | mesa_foot/bind aud key spelled (add (loadvar at len whole) (bex (mul 8 len))) foot
    ?> (./some root) (mesa_foot/mine aud foot) idle
    ? (eq kind 101)
      ?> (./some change) (mesa_foot/epic root name.at(2) name.at(3) state foot) idle
      = sealed (net/seal foot.seed root change)
        sent=[base=nat head=nat sig=nat lines=row[[rel=path stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat]]] (net/wired sealed)
        page (PinBytes (pin sent))
        shown (mesa_foot/show aud veiled sealed foot.shown)
      | mesa_foot/bind aud key spelled (mesa_foot/dress with spelled page) foot.set_shown(shown)
    ? !(eq kind 112) idle
    = at (mesa_foot/spot aud name.at(3))
      known
        ? foot.shown.has(at) foot.shown
        ?> (./some all) (mesa_foot/epic root 0 name.at(2) state foot) foot.shown
        | mesa_foot/show aud veiled all foot.shown
    ?> (./some item) known.get(at) (mesa_foot/served foot.set_shown(known) none)
    | mesa_foot/bind aud key spelled (mesa_foot/dress with spelled item.bytes) foot.set_shown(known)
  + dress
    ' This function gives a page as the foot sends it. The page is sealed when
    ' there is a peer to seal it for.
    \ with=maybe[mesa_foot/lock] spelled=nat page=nat
    ^ nat
    ?> (./some held) with page
    | mesa_foot/wrap held spelled page
  + refused_call
    ' This function tells if the engine did not accept a call at all.
    \ acts=row[row[nat]]
    ^ bool
    | acts.any((\ act=row[nat] (eq 5 act.at(0))))
  + bind
    ' This function binds a page under its name. A page is the bytes that Mesa
    ' binds to one name: here, an epic without its records, or one pin. Mesa
    ' sends one page as 1024 fragments of 1 KiB at most, and mesa_foot/page is
    ' the limit that the foot uses (1,000,000 bytes).
    '
    ' The foot keeps a page that is too long for Mesa to send as one page.
    ' Under the name of that page, the foot binds only its length and the
    ' number of slices that a reader can read it in. The foot binds each slice
    ' when a reader asks for it. If an engine has no space left, the foot
    ' replaces it with a new engine, one time only.
    \ aud=nat key=path spelled=nat page=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/served
    = none=row[row[nat]] []
      total (dec (wid page))
    ? (gt total mesa_foot/most_bytes) (mesa_foot/served foot none)
    ? (gt total mesa_foot/page)
      = count (div (add total (dec mesa_foot/page)) mesa_foot/page)
        fresh=mop[path nat lt_pith] (mop/empty)
        room (? (gt foot.kept.size 3) fresh foot.kept)
        holding foot.set_kept(room.put((mesa_foot/spot aud (Sha256 spelled)) page))
      | mesa_foot/bind aud key spelled (mesa_foot/slip total count) holding
    = ;acts (MesaBind foot.engine spelled page)
    ? !(mesa_foot/refused_call acts) (mesa_foot/served foot.set_bound(foot.bound.put(key 1)) acts)
    = again (mesa_foot/renew foot)
      ;second (MesaBind again.engine spelled page)
    ? (mesa_foot/refused_call second) (mesa_foot/served again none)
    | mesa_foot/served again.set_bound(again.bound.put(key 1)) second
  + slip
    ' This function gives the slip that is in the place of a long page. The
    ' slip is sixteen bytes that contain the word LONG, the number of bytes of
    ' the page, and the number of slices.
    \ total=nat count=nat
    ^ nat
    | add (add "LONG" (lsh total 32)) (add (lsh count 96) (bex 128))
  + slipped
    ' This function tells if bytes are such a slip. No page is a slip, because
    ' a stream starts with SILO and a sealed page is longer.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ bool
    ? !(eq 17 (wid bytes)) .false
    | eq "LONG" (mod bytes (bex 32))
  + read_page
    ' This function asks a peer for a page. When the name contains only an
    ' alias of the content hash, want is the content hash that the bytes of
    ' the page must have.
    \ from=nat kind=nat aud=nat a=nat b=nat want=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = request foot.next
      ;acts (MesaRead foot.engine request from (mesa_foot/name kind aud a b))
      asked foot.set_next(request.inc).set_asks(foot.asks.snoc((mesa_foot/ask request from kind aud a b want)))
    ? (mesa_foot/refused_call acts) (mesa_foot/finished request 1 0 state asked)
    | mesa_foot/acted acts state asked
  + acted
    ' This function does what an engine asks. It sends what the engine asks it
    ' to send. Then it does the step for each read that stopped.
    \ acts=row[row[nat]] state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = sent (mesa_foot/turn foot (mesa_foot/sends acts))
    | acts.fold sent
      \ acc=mesa_foot/turn act=row[nat]
      ^ mesa_foot/turn
      ? !(eq 2 act.at(0)) acc
      | mesa_foot/after acc
        \ now=mesa_foot/foot
        ^ mesa_foot/turn
        | mesa_foot/finished act.at(1) act.at(2) act.at(3) state now
  + home
    ' This function gives the card that this world keeps for itself.
    \ foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ path
    [foot.self 'io 'mesa]
  + hint
    \ to=mesa_foot/peer public=bool state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/effect
    | mesa_foot/hint_with to public 1 state foot
  + hint_with
    ' This function tells a peer the version that the root published to that
    ' peer is at. It also tells what this world holds of the same root of the
    ' peer. With each of the two, it tells how far each identity log goes. The
    ' receiver does not believe a hint: it compares and then reads. A hint can
    ' contain one statement of the log of this world, and that statement is
    ' its own proof.
    \ to=mesa_foot/peer public=bool word=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/effect
    = me (mesa_foot/id foot.self)
      root=path (? public [foot.self 'our 'public] [foot.self 'our to.world])
      theirs=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa to.world]
      offered (mesa_foot/head root state foot)
      held (net/held ['head] (mesa_foot/card to public foot) state)
      holds (mesa_foot/name 104 (? public 0 me) (net/held ['seq] theirs state) held)
      offers (mesa_foot/name 104 (? public 0 to.id) (net/held ['seq] (mesa_foot/home foot) state) offered)
    | mesa_foot/effect/send to.id (MesaHint mesa_foot/domain me to.id holds offers word mesa_foot/limits)
  + mark
    ' This function gives what a hint to a peer contains, as one number.
    \ to=mesa_foot/peer public=bool state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    = root=path (? public [foot.self 'our 'public] [foot.self 'our to.world])
      theirs=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa to.world]
      w64 18446744073709551616
      offered (mesa_foot/head root state foot)
      held (net/held ['head] (mesa_foot/card to public foot) state)
      logs (add (mul (net/held ['seq] (mesa_foot/home foot) state) 4294967296) (net/held ['seq] theirs state))
    | add (mul (add (mul offered w64) held) w64) logs
  + turned
    ' This function runs after the world commits. It sends a hint to each peer
    ' whose roots changed after the foot last told that peer. It also sends a
    ' hint when what this world holds of the roots of that peer changed.
    \ state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = both=row[bool] [.false .true]
    | foot.peers.fold (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
      \ acc=mesa_foot/turn item=mesa_foot/peer
      ^ mesa_foot/turn
      | both.fold acc
        \ had=mesa_foot/turn public=bool
        ^ mesa_foot/turn
        = at=path [($u item.id) ($u (? public 1 0))]
          now (mesa_foot/mark item public state had.foot)
        ? (eq now had.foot.told.get(at).fall(0)) had
        | mesa_foot/turn had.foot.set_told(had.foot.told.put(at now)) had.does.snoc((mesa_foot/hint item public state had.foot))
  + clock
    ' This function runs when time goes by. It lets the engine retry or stop
    ' all that the engine has in progress.
    \ now=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = ;acts (MesaAdvance foot.engine now)
    | mesa_foot/acted acts state foot
  + tick
    ' This function runs when time goes by and it is time to tell again the
    ' versions that the foot has. It tells each peer, and thus it repairs a
    ' lost hint.
    \ now=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = moved (mesa_foot/clock now state foot)
      hints
        | moved.foot.peers.fold moved.does
          \ out=row[mesa_foot/effect] item=mesa_foot/peer
          ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
          | out.snoc((mesa_foot/hint item .false state moved.foot)).snoc((mesa_foot/hint item .true state moved.foot)).weld((mesa_foot/brings item state moved.foot))
    | mesa_foot/turn moved.foot hints
  + brings
    ' This function gives what this world tells a peer that it is brought
    ' before. This applies while the card of this world for that peer holds
    ' the introduction in the slot bring. First it sends the log of the name
    ' that brings this world, when that name is a different name (this is the
    ' case for an invitation that this world claimed). This world keeps that
    ' log and sends it. Then it sends the introduction, and then each
    ' statement of the log of this world. Each hint contains one word.
    '
    ' It is possible that the peer still does not hear this world. Thus this
    ' world expects no answer, and it sends the same hints on each tick.
    \ to=mesa_foot/peer state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
    = none=row[mesa_foot/effect] []
      letter (net/held ['bring] (mesa_foot/card to .false foot) state)
    ? (eq 0 letter) none
    ?> (./some i) (pass/opened letter) none
    = home (mesa_foot/home foot)
      nothing (mesa_foot/name 104 0 0 0)
      nobody=row[pass/statement] []
      theirs=row[pass/statement]
        ? (eq i.by (mesa_foot/id foot.self)) nobody
        | pass/log (pass/beside home i.by) state
      carried
        | theirs.fold none
          \ out=row[mesa_foot/effect] s=pass/statement
          ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
          | out.snoc((mesa_foot/effect/send to.id (MesaHint mesa_foot/domain i.by to.id nothing nothing (pass/sent s) mesa_foot/limits)))
      said carried.snoc((mesa_foot/hint_with to .false letter state foot))
    | (pass/log home state).fold said
      \ out=row[mesa_foot/effect] s=pass/statement
      ^ row[mesa_foot/effect]
      | out.snoc((mesa_foot/hint_with to .false (pass/sent s) state foot))
  + busy
    ' This function tells if the foot already has a part of the root of a
    ' publisher in progress.
    \ from=nat aud=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ bool
    ? (foot.asks.any((\ item=mesa_foot/ask (and (eq item.from from) (eq item.aud aud))))) .true
    ? (foot.loads.any((\ item=mesa_foot/load (and (eq item.from from) (eq item.aud aud))))) .true
    | foot.lands.any((\ item=mesa_foot/landing (and (eq item.from from) (eq item.aud aud))))
  + taught
    ' This function runs when a packet contained a statement of the identity
    ' log of a name. It asks the world to put the statement on the card for
    ' that name. A statement is its own proof, and thus it is not important
    ' who sent it. The rules of the card do not accept a statement that does
    ' not follow, and such a statement changes no record.
    \ card=path word=nat had=mesa_foot/turn
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ?> (./some s) (pass/got word) had
    = plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | pass/turn card s now
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn had.foot had.does.weld(does)
  + introduced
    ' This function runs when a packet from a peer contained an introduction.
    ' In it, a different name brings a world that this world already hears, to
    ' get a second place. The foot asks the world to put the introduction on
    ' the card of the world brought. It does the same for the introduction of
    ' a stranger (pass/welcome). The function pass/welcome keeps the name that
    ' brought the world and does not change the address of that world. The
    ' peer sends the introduction on each tick. Thus this function does not
    ' use an introduction that the card already keeps, and it asks for no
    ' change.
    \ card=path word=nat state=namespace had=mesa_foot/turn
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ?> (./some i) (pass/opened word) had
    ? !(eq i.to (mesa_foot/id had.foot.self)) had
    ? (eq i.by i.name) had
    ? (eq word (net/held ['brought ($x i.by)] card state)) had
    = plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | pass/welcome card i now
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn had.foot had.does.weld(does)
  + hinted
    ' This function runs when a peer told the version that its root for this
    ' world is at. The peer also told what it holds of the root of this world.
    ' The function reads the root of the peer when its version is after the
    ' held version and was not refused. If the peer sent a statement of its
    ' identity log, the function accepts the statement. The function also
    ' accepts an introduction by a different name (introduced).
    '
    ' The function answers with a hint when the peer is behind on this world's
    ' root or on this world's identity log. When the peer is behind on that
    ' log, the answer contains the next statement that the peer does not have.
    ' The function also answers when the log of the peer is ahead of what this
    ' world holds of it, and the peer sent no statement. That answer tells how
    ' far this world goes, and the peer then sends the next statement.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer holds=nat offers=nat word=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = me (mesa_foot/id foot.self)
      offered (mesa_foot/parse offers)
      held (mesa_foot/parse holds)
      theirs=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa from.world]
      sought
        ? !(eq offered.len 4) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        ? !(eq offered.at(0) 104) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        = aud offered.at(1)
          public (eq aud 0)
        ? !((eq aud 0) || (eq aud me)) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        = card (mesa_foot/card from public foot)
        ? !(net/wants card offered.at(3) state) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        ? (mesa_foot/busy from.id aud foot) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
        | mesa_foot/read_page from.id 101 aud (net/held ['head] card state) offered.at(3) 0 state foot
      learned (mesa_foot/introduced theirs word state (mesa_foot/taught theirs word sought))
    ? !(eq held.len 4) learned
    ? !(eq held.at(0) 104) learned
    = aud held.at(1)
      public (eq aud 0)
    ? !((eq aud 0) || (eq aud from.id)) learned
    ?> (./some root) (mesa_foot/mine aud learned.foot) learned
    = home (mesa_foot/home foot)
      owed (gt (net/held ['seq] home state) held.at(2))
      next
        ? !owed 1
        ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at home held.at(2).inc) state) 1
        | pass/sent (pass/of record)
      lacking (and (and (eq offered.len 4) (lt (wid word) 2)) (gt offered.at(2) (net/held ['seq] theirs state)))
    ? !(((gt (mesa_foot/head root state learned.foot) held.at(3)) || owed) || lacking) learned
    | mesa_foot/turn learned.foot learned.does.snoc((mesa_foot/hint_with from public next state learned.foot))
  + told_self
    ' This function runs for a packet that names this world as its sender.
    ' From such a packet, the foot accepts only a statement of the identity
    ' log of this world. Custody sends the statement in this way, from any
    ' place where custody is kept.
    \ packet=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = seen (MesaLook packet mesa_foot/limits)
    ? !(eq 2 seen.at(0)) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    | mesa_foot/taught (mesa_foot/home foot) seen.at(3) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
  + stranger
    ' This function handles a hint that the foot does not accept as the hint
    ' of a peer. There are two such cases: the sender has no route in this
    ' world, or the hint is about a name that is not the name of its sender.
    '
    ' A hint can contain one word: a statement of an identity log, or an
    ' introduction. The foot reads that word and no other part of the hint.
    ' Each kind of word proves itself with its signatures. Thus the sender of
    ' the packet is not important, and any world can send the word on.
    '
    ' - A statement: the foot asks the world to put the statement on the card
    '   of the name that the hint is about. It does this only if that card
    '   already holds a log or an introduction. If the hint is about this
    '   world, the card is the record of the foot. This is how a statement
    '   from custody comes to a world, from each place that keeps the custody
    '   key.
    ' - An introduction that is addressed to this world: the foot asks the
    '   world to put the introduction on the card of the world that it
    '   brings. It does this only if this world holds a log for the name that
    '   brings that world.
    '
    ' Neither word makes this world hear the sender. A card is a route only
    ' when it holds a key and an address, and only a correct introduction
    ' writes the address (pass/welcome). The foot discards all other hints
    ' before it examines a signature.
    \ seen=row[nat] state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ? !(eq 2 seen.at(0)) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = word seen.at(3)
      mine (eq seen.at(4) (mesa_foot/id foot.self))
      other=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa ($x seen.at(4))]
      about=path (? mine (mesa_foot/home foot) other)
    ? (lt (wid word) 2) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    ?> (./some i) (pass/opened word) (mesa_foot/followed about word state foot)
    ? !(eq i.to (mesa_foot/id foot.self)) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = card=path [foot.self 'io 'mesa ($x i.name)]
      known=maybe[pass/standing]
        ? (eq i.by i.name) (pass/held card state)
        | pass/held (pass/beside card i.by) state
    ? !(maybe/is_some known) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | pass/welcome card i now
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn foot does
  + followed
    ' This function runs for a word that is not an introduction. The foot asks
    ' the world to put a statement on the card of the name that the statement
    ' is said to be about. It does this when this world keeps such a card.
    \ card=path word=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ?> (./some s) (pass/got word) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    ? !(net/live card state) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | pass/follow card s now
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn foot does
  + heard
    ' This function runs when a packet comes. The foot compares a hint from a
    ' peer. The foot lets an ask from a peer through only for a name that this
    ' world supplies to that peer. Such a name is for the public root or for
    ' the root published to that peer. All other packets are for the engine. A
    ' hint from a sender with no route, or about a name that is not its
    ' sender, is not the hint of a peer (refer to stranger).
    \ from=nat packet=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ? (eq from (mesa_foot/id foot.self)) (mesa_foot/told_self packet state foot)
    = seen (MesaLook packet mesa_foot/limits)
      kind seen.at(0)
    ? (eq kind 9) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    ?> (./some sender) (mesa_foot/peer_of from foot) (mesa_foot/stranger seen state foot)
    ? (and (eq kind 2) !(eq seen.at(4) from)) (mesa_foot/stranger seen state foot)
    ? (eq kind 2) (mesa_foot/hinted sender seen.at(1) seen.at(2) seen.at(3) state foot)
    ? !(eq kind 0) (mesa_foot/receive from packet mesa_foot/none state foot)
    = name (mesa_foot/parse seen.at(1))
    ? !(eq name.len 4) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    ? !((eq name.at(1) 0) || (eq name.at(1) from)) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = ready (mesa_foot/serve name seen.at(1) state foot)
    | mesa_foot/receive from packet ready.acts state ready.foot
  + none
    ^ row[row[nat]]
    []
  + receive
    \ from=nat packet=nat before=row[row[nat]] state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = ;acts (MesaReceive foot.engine from (mesa_foot/link from foot) packet)
    | mesa_foot/acted before.weld(acts) state foot
  + finished
    ' This function runs when a read stops, with its page or without it. A
    ' slice goes to the page that it is a slice of. A slip in the place of a
    ' page starts the read of the slices of that page. In all other cases, the
    ' bytes are the page.
    \ request=nat status=nat bytes=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = found foot.asks.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/ask (eq item.request request)))
    ? !(found.len) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = asked found.at(0)
      cleared foot.set_asks(foot.asks.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/ask !(eq item.request request))))
    ? (eq asked.kind 115) (mesa_foot/got_slice asked status bytes state cleared)
    ? (and (eq status 0) (mesa_foot/slipped bytes)) (mesa_foot/begin asked bytes state cleared)
    | mesa_foot/arrived asked status bytes state cleared
  + arrived
    ' This function runs when a complete page comes, or does not come. The
    ' foot opens a page of a peer's root with the key that this world shares
    ' with that peer. A page that does not open is the same as a page that did
    ' not come.
    \ asked=mesa_foot/ask status=nat bytes=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ?> (./some sender) (mesa_foot/peer_of asked.from foot) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = plain
        ? !(eq status 0) 0
        ? (eq 0 asked.aud) bytes
        ?> (./some with) (mesa_foot/lock_of asked.from foot) 0
        | mesa_foot/unwrap with (mesa_foot/name asked.kind asked.aud asked.a asked.b) bytes
      state_of (? (eq 0 plain) 1 status)
    ? (eq asked.kind 101) (mesa_foot/got_epic sender asked state_of plain state foot)
    | mesa_foot/got_pin sender asked state_of plain state foot
  + begin
    ' This function runs when a slip comes in the place of a page. It asks for
    ' the slices of that page, one window of slices at a time.
    \ asked=mesa_foot/ask slip=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = total (mod (rsh slip 32) (bex 64))
      count (mod (rsh slip 96) (bex 32))
      mark (Sha256 (mesa_foot/name asked.kind asked.aud asked.a asked.b))
      none=row[mesa_foot/stream] []
    ? (gt total mesa_foot/most_bytes) (mesa_foot/arrived asked 1 0 state foot)
    ? !(eq count (div (add total (dec mesa_foot/page)) mesa_foot/page)) (mesa_foot/arrived asked 1 0 state foot)
    | mesa_foot/more (mesa_foot/long asked mark total count 0 none) state foot
  + more
    ' This function asks for more slices of a long page, up to the window. The
    ' foot that the function gets does not hold this page, but the foot that
    ' the function gives holds it.
    \ reading=mesa_foot/long state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = flying (sub reading.asked reading.got.len)
      room (? (lt flying mesa_foot/window) (sub mesa_foot/window flying) 0)
      left (sub reading.count reading.asked)
      take (? (lt left room) left room)
      next reading.set_asked((add reading.asked take))
      holding foot.set_longs(foot.longs.snoc(next))
    % i=nat 0
      acc=mesa_foot/turn (mesa_foot/quiet holding)
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    ? (ge i take) acc
    | _ i.inc
      | mesa_foot/after acc
        \ now=mesa_foot/foot
        ^ mesa_foot/turn
        | mesa_foot/read_page reading.ask.from 115 reading.ask.aud reading.mark (add reading.asked i) 0 state now
  + got_slice
    ' This function runs when a slice comes, or does not come. When the last
    ' slice is in, the function puts the page together in order. It then uses
    ' the result as the page that the slices are the parts of.
    \ asked=mesa_foot/ask status=nat bytes=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = mine foot.longs.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/long (and (and (eq item.ask.from asked.from) (eq item.ask.aud asked.aud)) (eq item.mark asked.a))))
    ? !(mine.len) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = reading mine.at(0)
      rest foot.set_longs(foot.longs.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/long !(and (and (eq item.ask.from asked.from) (eq item.ask.aud asked.aud)) (eq item.mark asked.a)))))
    ? !(eq status 0) (mesa_foot/arrived reading.ask 1 0 state rest)
    = got reading.got.snoc((mesa_foot/stream asked.b bytes))
      next reading.set_got(got)
    ? (lt got.len reading.count) (mesa_foot/more next state rest)
    = whole (mesa_foot/joined got reading.count)
    ? !(eq (dec (wid whole)) reading.total) (mesa_foot/arrived reading.ask 1 0 state rest)
    | mesa_foot/arrived reading.ask 0 whole state rest
  + joined
    ' This function gives the slices put together in order, as one sequence of
    ' bytes. It makes a byte tree of the slices (bat_concat), and the runtime
    ' flattens the tree in one pass. Thus the work is in proportion to the
    ' number of bytes, and the function does no arithmetic on a large number
    ' for each slice.
    \ got=row[mesa_foot/stream] count=nat
    ^ nat
    % i=nat 0
      parts=row[any] []
    ^ nat
    ? (ge i count) (mesa_foot/flat parts)
    = found got.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/stream (eq item.hash i)))
    ? !(found.len) (mesa_foot/flat parts)
    | _ i.inc parts.snoc((bat_bar found.at(0).bytes))
  + flat
    ' This function gives the bytes of a row of byte trees as one bar: the
    ' bytes, and then the byte 1 that shows where they stop.
    \ parts=row[any]
    ^ nat
    | bat_flatten (bat_concat parts.snoc((bat_bytes 1 1)))
  + refused
    ' This function records on the card of the mount that a head was refused,
    ' and the reason. As a result, the foot does not ask for that head again.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer public=bool head=nat why=iota foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = card (mesa_foot/card from public foot)
      plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | either/ok[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]] (net/refuse card head why now)
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit 0 plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn foot does
  + got_epic
    ' This function runs when an epic comes, without its records. The foot
    ' makes no assumption about the epic. It must be an epic, the epic that
    ' the foot asked for, and the epic of its publisher. Then the foot asks
    ' for each record that this world does not have, by the hash of the
    ' record. A publisher that cannot give the change from the version that
    ' this world is at sends no page. The foot then asks that publisher for
    ' all of the root.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer asked=mesa_foot/ask status=nat bytes=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = public (eq asked.aud 0)
      idle (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    ? !(eq status 0)
      ? (eq asked.a 0) idle
      | mesa_foot/read_page from.id 101 asked.aud 0 asked.b 0 state foot
    = stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored idle
    ?> (./some change) (net/read (unpin held)) idle
    ? !(and (eq change.base asked.a) (eq change.head asked.b)) idle
    ? !(change.entries.all((\ item=net/entry ((eq 0 item.hash) || (mesa_foot/hashlike item.hash))))) idle
    = mount (mesa_foot/mount from public foot)
      card (mesa_foot/card from public foot)
      root (mesa_foot/theirs from public foot)
    ? !(net/proven (net/key card state) root change) (mesa_foot/refused from public change.head 'net_forged foot)
    = lacking (net/lacks mount change state)
      streams=row[mesa_foot/stream] []
      nothing=row[nat] []
      fresh (mesa_foot/load from.id asked.aud change streams nothing lacking 0)
    | mesa_foot/pump from fresh state foot
  + without
    ' This function keeps only the loads that are not for the root of one
    ' publisher.
    \ from=nat aud=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/foot
    | foot.set_loads(foot.loads.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/load !(and (eq item.from from) (eq item.aud aud)))))
  + got_pin
    ' This function runs when the bytes of a pin come. The bytes must hash to
    ' the name that the foot used to ask for them. The foot then asks for each
    ' pin that the bytes name. When no pin is left, the epic is complete. When
    ' a page does not come, the foot does not continue with this epic, and a
    ' later hint starts again.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer asked=mesa_foot/ask status=nat bytes=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = public (eq asked.aud 0)
      held foot.loads.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/load (and (and (eq item.from asked.from) (eq item.aud asked.aud)) (eq item.epic.head asked.a))))
    ? !(held.len) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = gathering held.at(0)
      rest (mesa_foot/without asked.from asked.aud foot)
    ? !(eq status 0) (mesa_foot/quiet rest)
    ? !(eq (Sha256 bytes) asked.want) (mesa_foot/refused from public asked.a 'net_unfit rest)
    = streams=row[mesa_foot/stream] gathering.streams.snoc((mesa_foot/stream asked.want bytes))
      size (add gathering.size (wid bytes))
    ? (gt streams.len mesa_foot/most) (mesa_foot/refused from public asked.a 'net_unfit rest)
    ? (gt size mesa_foot/room) (mesa_foot/refused from public asked.a 'net_unfit rest)
    = still=row[nat] gathering.wanted.keep((\ hash=nat !(eq hash asked.want)))
      named=row[nat] (PinNames bytes)
      more=row[nat]
        | named.keep
          \ hash=nat
          ^ bool
          ? (streams.any((\ item=mesa_foot/stream (eq item.hash hash)))) .false
          ? (still.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen hash)))) .false
          ? (gathering.queued.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen hash)))) .false
          .true
      next gathering.set_streams(streams).set_wanted(still).set_queued(gathering.queued.weld(more)).set_size(size)
    | mesa_foot/pump from next state rest
  + pump
    ' This function asks for more of the pages of an epic, up to the window.
    ' When the foot waits for no page and no page is left to ask for, the epic
    ' is complete. The foot that the function gets does not hold this load.
    ' The foot that the function gives holds the load as the load is now.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer gathering=mesa_foot/load state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = room (? (lt gathering.wanted.len mesa_foot/window) (sub mesa_foot/window gathering.wanted.len) 0)
      count (? (lt gathering.queued.len room) gathering.queued.len room)
      asking=row[nat] gathering.queued.take(count)
      next gathering.set_wanted(gathering.wanted.weld(asking)).set_queued(gathering.queued.drop(count))
      loading foot.set_loads(foot.loads.snoc(next))
      veiled
        ? (eq 0 gathering.aud) 0
        ?> (./some held) (mesa_foot/lock_of from.id foot) 0
        held.name
    ? (and (eq 0 next.wanted.len) (eq 0 next.queued.len)) (mesa_foot/complete from next state loading)
    | asking.fold (mesa_foot/quiet loading)
      \ acc=mesa_foot/turn hash=nat
      ^ mesa_foot/turn
      | mesa_foot/after acc
        \ now=mesa_foot/foot
        ^ mesa_foot/turn
        | mesa_foot/read_page from.id 112 gathering.aud gathering.epic.head (mesa_foot/veil veiled hash) hash state now
  + staged
    ' This function tells if a stream loads into the store now. A stream loads
    ' after each pin that it names loads. A stream that holds code does not
    ' load.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ bool
    = found=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    | maybe/is_some found
  + rebuild
    ' This function loads each stream, and it loads the parts of a pin before
    ' the pin. Parts come after the pin that names them, and thus the last
    ' streams that came go first. The function tries again all that a pass
    ' does not load, while a pass still loads some stream.
    \ streams=row[mesa_foot/stream]
    ^ bool
    % left=row[mesa_foot/stream] streams.rev
    ^ bool
    ? !(left.len) .true
    = ;after (left.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/stream !(mesa_foot/staged item.bytes))))
    ? (eq after.len left.len) .false
    | _ after
  + record
    ' This function gives the record that a stream holds, when the stream
    ' holds a record.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[myth]
    = none=maybe[myth] .none
      found=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) found none
    | net/sound (unpin held)
  + plan
    ' This function lands an epic. If the rules do not accept the epic because
    ' of what it is, the function records that on the card instead. This does
    ' not apply when the rules do not accept the epic because of when it came.
    \ mount=path card=path change=net/epic now=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (net/land mount card change now)
     > (./ok writes) (either/ok[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]] writes)
     > (./err why)
      ? (eq_iota why 'net_stale) (./err why)
      ? (eq_iota why 'net_gap) (./err why)
      | either/ok[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]] (net/refuse card change.head why now)
  + complete
    ' This function runs after all the streams of an epic came. It makes the
    ' pins of the epic again. It puts each record where the epic names the
    ' hash of that record. Then it asks the world to land the epic as one
    ' event.
    \ from=mesa_foot/peer gathered=mesa_foot/load state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = public (eq gathered.aud 0)
      rest (mesa_foot/without gathered.from gathered.aud foot)
      head gathered.epic.head
      ;whole (mesa_foot/rebuild gathered.streams)
    ? !whole (mesa_foot/refused from public head 'net_unfit rest)
    = start=maybe[net/epic] (./some gathered.epic)
      none=maybe[net/epic] .none
      filled
        | gathered.epic.entries.fold start
          \ acc=maybe[net/epic] item=net/entry
          ^ maybe[net/epic]
          ?> (./some change) acc none
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) acc
          = found gathered.streams.keep((\ got=mesa_foot/stream (eq got.hash item.hash)))
          ? !(found.len) acc
          ?> (./some record) (mesa_foot/record found.at(0).bytes) none
          | ./some (net/given item.hash record change)
    ?> (./some change) filled (mesa_foot/refused from public head 'net_unfit rest)
    = mount (mesa_foot/mount from public foot)
      card (mesa_foot/card from public foot)
      request rest.next
      plan
        \ now=namespace
        ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
        | mesa_foot/plan mount card change now
      waiting rest.set_next(request.inc).set_lands(rest.lands.snoc((mesa_foot/landing request gathered.from gathered.aud head)))
      does=row[mesa_foot/effect] [(mesa_foot/effect/admit request plan)]
    | mesa_foot/turn waiting does
  + landed
    ' This function runs when the world gives an answer to a landing. If the
    ' kernel did not accept the landing, the function records that on the card
    ' with the reason. An example is a landing with a record that does not
    ' obey its own contract.
    \ request=nat why=maybe[iota] foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ mesa_foot/turn
    = found foot.lands.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/landing (eq item.request request)))
    ? !(found.len) (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
    = was found.at(0)
      cleared foot.set_lands(foot.lands.keep((\ item=mesa_foot/landing !(eq item.request request))))
    ?> (./some reason) why (mesa_foot/quiet cleared)
    ? (eq_iota reason 'net_stale) (mesa_foot/quiet cleared)
    ? (eq_iota reason 'net_gap) (mesa_foot/quiet cleared)
    ?> (./some sender) (mesa_foot/peer_of was.from cleared) (mesa_foot/quiet cleared)
    | mesa_foot/refused sender (eq was.aud 0) was.head reason cleared
